COPD Exacerbation
Respiratory System
Increased dyspnea, Increased sputum volume, Increased sputum purulence

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-012
Reason for Visit: Acute deterioration in respiratory status characterized by increased sputum purulence and severe dyspnea, failing to respond to home rescue inhalers.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an acute worsening of baseline dyspnea and increased use of accessory muscles in a patient with severe chronic lung disease. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to initiate systemic steroids and antibiotics to prevent hypercapnic respiratory failure before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My chest is completely blocked with green phlegm, and my puffers are not doing anything to help me breathe.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have escalated over the past 3 days, marking an acute worsening of baseline dyspnea. The patient notes significantly increased sputum volume and increased sputum purulence, shifting from clear to dark green, strongly indicating a bacterial trigger. The cough severity has worsened, disrupting sleep. The patient reports increased wheezing, severe fatigue, and borderline confusion noted by the adult child over the last few hours.
Functional Status: Usually independent with a walker; currently chair-bound and struggling to speak in full sentences.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with an adult child; high risk of hospitalization if not stabilized quickly.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/88 mmHg | HR: 108 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 26 breaths/min | SpO2: 88% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Distressed, sitting forward in a tripod position, utilizing accessory muscles of respiration, prominent audible rhonchi.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals poor air entry globally with widespread expiratory wheeze and coarse rhonchi. Worsening hypoxemia is noted on pulse oximetry, with mild central cyanosis. Cardiovascular exam shows tachycardia. The patient appears fatigued with mild altered mental status, raising concern for early hypercapnia.
Environmental Observations: Multiple inhalers scattered on the table; home oxygen concentrator present but poorly optimized.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has overused short-acting bronchodilators today without a spacer.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: COPD Exacerbation - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sputum is distinctly purulent (green) rather than frothy pink, and history points strongly to infective trigger.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual 3-day escalation of purulent sputum and absence of sudden pleuritic pain.

Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of focal unilateral consolidation signs or high spiking fevers.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the cardinal symptoms of an infective COPD exacerbation: acutely increased dyspnea, increased sputum volume, and increased sputum purulence. The presence of tachycardia, tachypnea, accessory muscle use, and borderline altered mental status signifies impending hypercapnic respiratory failure. Waiting until the next in-hours period for assessment would inevitably lead to severe respiratory acidosis and the need for non-invasive ventilation or intubation. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to aggressively deliver nebulized bronchodilators, initiate systemic corticosteroids, and commence targeted oral antibiotics.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered continuous nebulized Salbutamol/Ipratropium. Prescribed oral Prednisolone 50mg daily for 5 days and Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg twice daily. Titrated home oxygen to achieve SpO2 88-92%.
Positive Behavioral Change: Place the antibiotic and steroid pill bottles directly on top of the coffee machine; linking the medication to your established morning routine ensures 100% compliance, directly accelerating lung recovery.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral for post-exacerbation pulmonary rehabilitation.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow; early morning appointment required to reassess respiratory drive and oxygenation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately for ambulance transport to the Emergency Department if the patient becomes unusually sleepy, confused, or if the oxygen saturations drop below 88% despite treatment.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis requires close monitoring; an arterial blood gas may be necessary in the hospital if confusion worsens to check carbon dioxide levels.
Clinical Course: The breathing will slowly ease over 48 hours as the steroids reduce swelling and the antibiotics clear the bacterial infection.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Family must call 000 immediately if the patient becomes difficult to wake up, mumbles their words, or if their breathing becomes slow and shallow.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The bacterial infection caused massive swelling in your damaged airways; the antibiotics kill the bacteria, and the steroids rapidly shrink the swelling so air can move again.
Actionable Steps: Keep your oxygen flow strictly at the level I set; turning the oxygen up higher will actually stop your brain from telling your lungs to breathe, causing dangerous carbon dioxide build-up.

